1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
And which is the one right way to find out whether people are overconfident?

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:06,000
I don't know.

3
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:08,000
There's two different approaches which give two different answers.

4
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:11,000
It seems reasonable to me to split the difference and say,

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
kind of seems like it, but maybe not.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Or, of course, maybe it is highly subject specific.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Like people are very overconfident in their political claims.

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:31,000
But maybe not so overconfident in, say, whether or not they're going to be getting a raise next week or something like that.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Also possible.

10
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Alright, so questions about that?

11
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Yes, Nathan?

12
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
Has there been anything on the interaction between this sort of epistemic overconfidence and social desirability bias?

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,000
That's a great question, yeah.

14
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:55,000
It might look really bad to say, oh, well I'm not sure if this thing is a bad thing.

15
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Yeah, I don't know if your dog can be saved by the vet.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
I'm sure the vet will be able to take care of this.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
That's what a good person says.

18
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
An honest person who says, I don't know, he looks incurable to me, I think you're just wasting your money.

19
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Not a nice thing to say.

20
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:23,000
According to society, although according to true concern for another person,

21
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
say, well look, there's a question, can you say the truth with sympathy?

22
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:37,000
Like, I know a lot about veterinary medicine, there's nothing they can say to your dog, and that person is just taking advantage of you.

23
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:39,000
Try that.

24
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Might work.

25
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:45,000
You can't give up on my dog.

26
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Who are you to say whether a dog will live or die?

27
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:54,000
Doctor of veterinary medicine, qualified as anyone, specializing in this very disease.

28
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:56,000
No one will get to convince me.

29
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
So there's that mentality, which you may have seen in plenty of cases.

30
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:05,000
So yeah, I don't know if anyone saw that.

31
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,000
It seems very plausible, and maybe that could be a dissertation.

32
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Who knows?

33
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:13,000
But anyway, so interaction between social desirability bias and overconfidence.

34
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:22,000
And sort of along those lines, I'd be curious to see what work's been done regarding the sort of general overconfidence,

35
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:28,000
underconfidence of professional researchers and practitioners in economics, medicine, and whatnot.

36
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:36,000
You think of episodes like how forever we thought that fat was the problem, and now it's carbs.

37
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:39,000
And social desirability bias.

38
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
So once people say, it's the fat that makes you fat, and like any time you say, what?

39
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:47,000
The fat makes you fat.

40
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Once that saying becomes socially accepted and standard, then anyone saying something else,

41
00:02:53,000 --> 00:02:56,000
it's almost like you're saying, so you want people to be fat, huh?

42
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
You just think it's great for people to die of obesity.

43
00:02:58,000 --> 00:02:59,000
That's the way.

44
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:01,000
I'm saying that's the wrong theory.

45
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
What's the wrong theory?

46
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:16,000
We're supposed to like, when did you become a paid disciple of big fat?

47
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,000
So real-world significance.

48
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:24,000
This is something where, over time I have moved the view that this matters more than I initially thought.

49
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:26,000
I am not constantly moving this direction.

50
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
I stabilized a while back.

51
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:37,000
But some of the experiments show that you've got a lot of problems, but what real-world mistakes can be attributed to them?

52
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:43,000
So like in the week where we do public choice, I'll mention, I think that this describes political views so well,

53
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
and so painfully well, just to see the opinions that people have on subjects that they couldn't possibly know much of anything about.

54
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Just the way that everybody knows that elasticities work the way they want them to work,

55
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:01,000
and they know it before they even know what elasticity they need for the answer.

56
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:06,000
So imagine you're teaching someone elasticity, and then before that, say,

57
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:10,000
so what do you think elasticity of labor demand is, high or low?

58
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:16,000
And until you have actually got them to think about, well, which answer applies to my view on the minimum wage is true,

59
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
people will probably give you one answer.

60
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
It's like, well, I don't know.

61
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:21,000
It doesn't seem like that.

62
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Why would I have an opinion about the elasticity of labor demand?

63
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:26,000
And then you say, well, if labor demand is highly inelastic,

64
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:30,000
it means the minimum wage doesn't cause much unemployment, but it's highly elastic when it does.

65
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:33,000
Well, then, man, it's so inelastic.

66
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:35,000
It's highly inelastic, man.

67
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
And this is one you could actually go and try tricking people and actually just tell them one or the other

68
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
and see if they just leap to whatever elasticity assumption gets them the result they want.

69
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:52,000
I mean, it might be too hard to actually do experimentally because it would involve an elasticity lecture, but still.

70
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:55,000
It seems like it's interesting.

71
00:04:55,000 --> 00:04:59,000
I guess it would even work if you just said, like, what was the slope of the demand curve?

72
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:02,000
That would also give the same qualitative result.

73
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:03,000
Was there a question, Nathan?

74
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:09,000
Might that be reflective of them, to be charitable to people who are displaying this bias,

75
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
of them knowing something about the empirical literature but not understanding the underlying economics?

76
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
So if someone thinks, oh, well, I know there's a well-done, hard-cruiter-like study,

77
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,000
I'm going to say that probably the data, the elasticity reflects what is shown in that data.

78
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Yes, that would be perfectly fine if you had some independent reason of thinking that the hard-cruiter study was accurate

79
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:33,000
other than that it gave you the result you wanted.

80
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,000
Although, in order to do that, you would have to understand elasticity in the first place, so it seems unlikely.

81
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:40,000
But theoretically, of course, it's possible.

82
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:45,000
I mean, just imagine that someone knows an incredibly trustworthy person who just reads a lot of things

83
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
and just tells them the answers.

84
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:51,000
Any time that they audit the person, what they say is perfectly accurate, that would be fine.

85
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
But how many people actually have a person like that in their lives?

86
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,000
Where are these people?

87
00:05:56,000 --> 00:05:58,000
My roommate. At least he tells me he's always right.

88
00:05:58,000 --> 00:05:59,000
He tells you he's right.

89
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:04,000
So the auditing is important, where you have to at least once in a while just double-check that what they're saying is right

90
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:08,000
so that you aren't simply saying, well, look, how accurate are you?

91
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Totally accurate.

92
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:10,000
Okay, then.

93
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:11,000
You're like, well, great.

94
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:13,000
Now let's go and ask you all the questions.

95
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
I mean, it answers everything for this one person who's told me it's totally accurate.

96
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:19,000
All right.

97
00:06:19,000 --> 00:06:25,000
So now something else you can say about belief perseverance is once you have a lot of evidence,

98
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:28,000
it is reasonable to not update much anymore.

99
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
So that can be true, and yet once you've seen 1,000 observations,

100
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:39,000
it's very reasonable to not noticeably budge when 1,000 first observations comes along.

101
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
So I have the following arrogant view about the news.

102
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:46,000
Look, I have spent 40 years reading history.

103
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:50,000
I understand the kinds of things that happen in the world,

104
00:06:50,000 --> 00:06:54,000
and the stuff in the news is in no way surprising to me at all, really.

105
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:59,000
It's just all the same kind of junk that I expect to happen given everything I've read before.

106
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:04,000
So if someone says, you're so dogmatic you don't change your mind on terrorism in light of yesterday's news.

107
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Do you know how many events like that have occurred in history?

108
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,000
Tens of thousands.

109
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:14,000
So why should your event make any more difference to me than like a fly landing on my nose?

110
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
It doesn't change my view of anything.

111
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:23,000
People don't like this attitude, but it seems like a reasonable attitude if you are that well informed, of course.

112
00:07:23,000 --> 00:07:27,000
It is tempting to claim to be super well informed long before you actually are,

113
00:07:27,000 --> 00:07:30,000
because these are the kinds of things that you can go and check for yourself.

114
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:36,000
If you say, oh, well, let me go and get a history test and see how well I can do off the top of my head.

115
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,000
I have a 1% time.

116
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:40,000
Pretty good, actually.

117
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:41,000
All right, good.

118
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:43,000
All right, my arrogance is justified.

119
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:48,000
All right, and then, you know, like issues as emotional as death penalty are rare,

120
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:50,000
so maybe that's not such a great example either.

121
00:07:50,000 --> 00:07:55,000
So I'm blanking on his name, but there's, so since doing this, there's been a psychologist

122
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:59,000
that's been doing a lot of work on political beliefs and just how ridiculous they are,

123
00:07:59,000 --> 00:08:04,000
and he has found that like on many issues,

124
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:10,000
this general fact that people will just go and wait and will just reverse the reasoning from is the right answer,

125
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:13,000
then that determines how reliable the evidence is.

126
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:15,000
And is the person an expert?

127
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:16,000
That too.

128
00:08:16,000 --> 00:08:17,000
Well, what is their answer?

129
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,000
In that case, I can only know their expertise based upon their agreement.

130
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:25,000
So climatologist at Harvard, well, does he agree with me on global warming?

131
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:26,000
If so, he's an expert.

132
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,000
If not, he's not an expert.

133
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:30,000
Right, that mentality is fairly pronounced,

134
00:08:30,000 --> 00:08:35,000
and he does a lot of experimental manipulations where you can really see that people are just going

135
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:39,000
and doing something that is extremely correct,

136
00:08:39,000 --> 00:08:43,000
where it's all just reasoning backwards from the answer that you want,

137
00:08:43,000 --> 00:08:47,000
and Bayes' rule is not being allowed to have any say.

138
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:49,000
All right, questions?

139
00:08:49,000 --> 00:08:53,000
All right, then let's move on to more fun stuff.

140
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:59,000
All right, one of the ways that people often ask me probabilities is by thinking about the E,

141
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,000
is by the E's of thinking of examples.

142
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:06,000
It's like, what's the probability the next person who walks in the room is Albanian?

143
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Well, one thing you might do is how easy, how many Albanians can I think of?

144
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:16,000
And if it's like no Albanians, then probably you're going to get a low probability.

145
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:21,000
If it's like, my God, there's tons of Albanians, then it's going to be high probability.

146
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,000
That's the idea.

147
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:25,000
All right, now this has a special name.

148
00:09:25,000 --> 00:09:27,000
It's called the availability heuristic.

149
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:35,000
The availability heuristic.

150
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:40,000
All right, and very often it's an extremely useful heuristic.

151
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:51,000
All right, so what are the odds that the next movie that becomes a bestseller in the U.S. is a Romanian movie?

152
00:09:51,000 --> 00:10:01,000
I can think of zero Romanian movies that have ever appeared in the list of the top grossing movies of the week.

153
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:07,000
So I'm going to put that really low.

154
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:10,000
And on the other hand, what about a Tom Cruise movie?

155
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:15,000
Probably the Tom Cruise movie becomes one of the top ten grossing movies.

156
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,000
Well, let's see.

157
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:21,000
So, four movies a year, and how many movies are there?

158
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
And they all are in the top ten pretty much.

159
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:26,000
So, that's going to be, what?

160
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:30,000
Maybe like 200 theatrical releases a year, four Tom Cruise movies.

161
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:34,000
So, that's going to be like one in 50.

162
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:36,000
That's a 2% chance.

163
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:41,000
And actually, we know it's even higher because movies generally stay in the top ten for more than one week.

164
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
So, if it's just for one week, then it should be even higher than that.

165
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:44,000
Yeah, Ash?

166
00:10:44,000 --> 00:10:47,000
Can you explain maybe the difference between heuristic and bias?

167
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:49,000
Ah, I'm getting to that.

168
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:50,000
I'm getting to that.

169
00:10:50,000 --> 00:10:51,000
Okay.

170
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:52,000
Alright.

171
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,000
So, anyway, this is known as the availability heuristic.

172
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:57,000
Just trying to sort of think, how can I think of examples?

173
00:10:57,000 --> 00:10:59,000
Well, lots of examples, likely.

174
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,000
Not many examples, not likely.

175
00:11:02,000 --> 00:11:03,000
Okay.

176
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,000
Alright.

177
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:09,000
Now, sometimes very useful, but it also predictably generates bias judgments.

178
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:19,000
And what it does, when the heuristic predictably leads us in the wrong direction, then we call it availability bias.

179
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:22,000
So, heuristic could be useful, and in fact, often is.

180
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
The bias is when the heuristic is hackable.

181
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:30,000
When you can hack the heuristic, or when the world hacks the heuristic,

182
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:37,000
and takes something that normally works and turns it into something that leads you very predictably into error.

183
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,000
So, for example, if an event is especially vivid or memorable,

184
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:44,000
then there's a lot of evidence that we overrate the probabilities,

185
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,000
because it is easy to remember vivid and memorable events.

186
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:50,000
Memorable events are, of course, by definition, easy to remember.

187
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:51,000
Right?

188
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:52,000
Vivid events.

189
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:54,000
Vivid events are just more memorable.

190
00:11:54,000 --> 00:11:56,000
So, odds of dying in a plane crash.

191
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,000
You can probably think of a number of famous plane crashes.

192
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:03,000
On the other hand, famous car crashes, not too many.

193
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:08,000
So, that's one where the availability heuristic, which normally is reasonable,

194
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:12,000
but because plane crashes are so vivid, so gripping to people,

195
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:19,000
that it actually seems to predictably lead people to overestimate the probability of dying in a plane crash relative to a car crash.

196
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:23,000
Car crashes are actually much more, cars are much more dangerous per mile.

197
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
So, you remember economists saying,

198
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:28,000
you shouldn't require babies to have a seatbelt on planes,

199
00:12:28,000 --> 00:12:34,000
because more babies will die if, based upon our estimates of price elasticity demand for flying versus driving.

200
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:37,000
So, basically, if you require every baby to have a seat,

201
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
then it's more expensive to fly with a baby,

202
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
which will lead to more people driving their babies,

203
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:44,000
and since driving is more dangerous than flying, more babies die.

204
00:12:44,000 --> 00:12:47,000
Alright, that's the kind of reasoning economists do.

205
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:50,000
But, non-economists, on the other hand, just think about,

206
00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:54,000
oh my god, it's so terrible when a plane crashes, they're so scary,

207
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:56,000
and so that means that it's a real danger.

208
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:00,000
And then, we got into what is car crashes, how often does that happen?

209
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:03,000
Alright, well, I don't know, I can't think of any good cases.

210
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:07,000
Certainly, the only time a car crashes on the front page of the news is,

211
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:11,000
just a regular car crash, not trying to kill a bunch of people in a car,

212
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:17,000
but a car accident, the only time it's on the front page of the news is when?

213
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:21,000
When a celebrity dies in a car crash.

214
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:25,000
But, no normal human being dying in a car crash would ever be news.

215
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:30,000
Whereas, if one person fell out of a plane somehow, that would be front page news.

216
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:36,000
Right, and they're talking about, oh, look, how are we letting the airlines get away

217
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:39,000
with these insecure planes where a person can just fall out?

218
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:44,000
It's happened once. What makes you think we're letting them get away with anything?

219
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:47,000
How many people have flown in planes, one fell out?

220
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:50,000
Is that a lot? Seems like a very small number.

221
00:13:50,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Alright, but that's not the kind of reaction that you're going to see,

222
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:57,000
and of course, it's very vivid thinking about a person falling out of a plane.

223
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:01,000
That's terrifying.

224
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:07,000
I'm getting a slight, my palms, the palms of the soles of my feet are getting slightly sweaty

225
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:11,000
just thinking about a person falling out of a plane.

226
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:15,000
Whenever I see a mountain climbing movie, wipe the sweat off.

227
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
So vivid. Whereas, I see people who get electrocuted, it doesn't bother me at all.

228
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:24,000
I don't have any reaction at all when someone's getting electrocuted,

229
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:32,000
but falling, oh my God, like, the sweat, it's scary how much sweat I can make.

230
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,000
Why that is, I don't know.

231
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:38,000
Seems like it's kind of dysfunctional for people to get really sweaty palms

232
00:14:38,000 --> 00:14:44,000
when they're thinking about falling from a height, but I don't know why.

233
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,000
So one possibility is just that they're very vivid,

234
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:51,000
but there's other cases where it's not so much vivid as it's just easier

235
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:54,000
for the human mind to go and do the search.

236
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:56,000
So think about this.

237
00:14:56,000 --> 00:14:59,000
So there are more words in the dictionary that start with the letter A

238
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:03,000
or that have the letter I in the third to last position of the word.

239
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:06,000
Well, if you're thinking about words that start with A, that's super easy, right?

240
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,000
Apple, anchor, you know, they just go on and on.

241
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:16,000
But on the other hand, words with I in the third to last letter.

242
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:17,000
Yes.

243
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:18,000
Yes.

244
00:15:18,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Yes.

245
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:22,000
So, you know, it's hard until you hit on the trick,

246
00:15:22,000 --> 00:15:26,000
which is any word that ends with I and G qualifies.

247
00:15:26,000 --> 00:15:30,000
Singing, running, dancing, leaping, talking, right, then you can get a ton.

248
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:34,000
But if you don't get that trick, you may sit there, you know, like,

249
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:36,000
you can't think of any.

250
00:15:36,000 --> 00:15:37,000
There's got to be some, right?

251
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:38,000
This is a trick question.

252
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:42,000
There's actually no such words, but there are.

253
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:47,000
All right, so people find it much easier to do a mental search based upon first letters

254
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
than third to last letters.

255
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
Notice a computer would have no trouble doing both equally well.

256
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
A human being is a different story.

257
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:57,000
A human being is used to thinking about words by their first letters.

258
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:00,000
So, all right, so anyway, people, it's much easier to come up with those,

259
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:05,000
and people normally falsely conclude that words that start with the letter A

260
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
are more probable in the dictionary, are more probable.

261
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
So to readily pick a word out of the dictionary, it's more likely to start with A.

262
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:13,000
It's actually more likely to buy in the third to last position.

263
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
Okay.

264
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:20,000
So availability bias is often used to explain why people overestimate the risk of flying,

265
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:21,000
for example.

266
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:22,000
All right.

267
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:25,000
So, that's out there.

268
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
Questions about availability bias?

269
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:28,000
Yes.

270
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:34,000
So would it be correct to say that availability bias is just incorrect availability heuristic?

271
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:39,000
Yeah, well, so, what I would just say is that

272
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:42,000
most availability heuristics are reasonable most of the time,

273
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:49,000
and the bias is in like the subset where there's some particular way that the human mind screws up

274
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
so that it's misleading.

275
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:56,000
It could be that for some issues, the heuristic is just a bias,

276
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
where it just always leads you in the wrong direction.

277
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:04,000
It could be that we just happen to be in an unusual environment where it's wrong,

278
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:07,000
or, again, it could just be that it normally works fine,

279
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:12,000
but there's just a narrow subset that gets heavily researched because we're trying to improve on our views.

280
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:16,000
Or, of course, there's also the possibility that it's confirmation bias,

281
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:21,000
and psychologists really want to show that human beings are wrong about everything,

282
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:25,000
and so they're looking around for every time human beings screw up to go and say,

283
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
see, further proof!

284
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
Human beings can't do anything right.

285
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:32,000
So there's that possibility, too.

286
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:38,000
To me, the most obvious case of availability bias in the world is our focus on terrorism.

287
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:41,000
Actual quantitative severity of terrorism.

288
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
Well, basic numbers.

289
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:47,000
Fraction of all murders that are terrorism on Earth.

290
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
Under 1%.

291
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
Fraction of all deaths that are murders.

292
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
Under 1%.

293
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:58,000
Terrorism causes under 1% of 1% of deaths.

294
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Less than 1 in 10,000.

295
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:07,000
How much coverage and news space does terrorism get relative to its actual severity?

296
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:11,000
A hundred times more than it should get based upon the ability to risk.

297
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:16,000
I think even that's probably more like a thousand times what it actually deserves.

298
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:19,000
And, of course, there's no policy response to this error, right?

299
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:24,000
Instead, over a trillion dollars spent fighting terrorism,

300
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:31,000
a problem that is less severe than groundings in bathtubs.

301
00:18:31,000 --> 00:18:38,000
And, again, if you want to talk about social desirability bias here,

302
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:41,000
you're running for office saying what I said.

303
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:45,000
Look, we're doing way too much about a very statistically small problem.

304
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:49,000
There are, in the world, pretty much almost any problem you've ever heard of,

305
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:51,000
and most of the problems you haven't are worse.

306
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:56,000
So, what are you talking about?

307
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
So, you don't care about terrorism.

308
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
I care about it appropriately.

309
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:03,000
I care about it appropriately.

310
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:06,000
Well, you're making a joke about horrible deaths.

311
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:11,000
Well, your deal is to be like, it's not a joke.

312
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:15,000
I'm making a joke out about how bad people are at estimating probabilities

313
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:20,000
and how much people allow their emotions to color their judgments.

314
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
You say, that's fair. That's funny.

315
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
The actual things aren't funny.

316
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:27,000
But if someone thought that dying from choking on chewing gum

317
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:31,000
was the biggest risk in the world, that would be pretty funny.

318
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:34,000
It's not funny that it happens.

319
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
It's funny that someone could be so wrong.

320
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:40,000
And even funnier that it's such a widespread error.

